WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.750
INTRO:
I’m sure we’ve all seen this warning message

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:03.750
in the past.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.720
“Please note that WinRAR is not free software.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.480
After a 40-day trial period you must either
buy a license or remove it from your computer.”

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.220
But the funny part is that, even after the
40-day trial period expires, nothing happens.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.150
WinRAR doesn’t block you from using the
program nor do they force you uninstall the

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.330
software.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.999
In fact, you can just close this window and
continue using WinRAR like nothing happened.

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:31.970
You don’t have to keep downloading new trial
versions of WinRAR or find a cracked version

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:32.970
of the software.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.780
But, I’m sure all of ya’ll already knew
that because this is precisely what everyone

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:38.780
does.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.710
This isn’t some sort of glitch either, the
company behind WinRAR is well aware of this

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.300
situation and they actively allow users to
easily bypass their pay wall.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.079
It’s also not like WinRAR runs any ads or
sells your data, so they truly don’t make

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:55.480
any money from users who refuse to buy a license.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.760
Considering this, why was this virtually free
software created, who actually ends up paying,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.149
and how does WinRAR even make money?

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:09.010
HISTORY:
Well, starting off with the history of WinRAR,

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.190
WinRAR is by no means a new piece of software.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:17.230
WinRAR was launched way back on April 22,
1995 by two brothers named Eugene and Alexander

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.240
Roshal.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.350
Eugene was the brains behind the operation
and took care of all the coding behind WinRAR.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.860
Meanwhile, Alexander worked on the marketing
and distribution of the software.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.880
By trade, Eugene was a software engineer,
so he was well aware of all of the strengths

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.960
and weaknesses of early computers.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.280
And one of their biggest weaknesses was limited
storage.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.570
The obvious solution for most people was to
simply buy more storage when they ran out,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.420
but Eugene felt that there was a better solution.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.570
What if you could have the same data take
up less space.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.090
This wasn’t a new idea by any means, but
Eugene had a special twist on the idea.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.950
You see, the vast majority of data compression
software that was available in the 1980s was

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.680
lossy based compression.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.040
As the name suggests, this means that part
of the data is lost during compression.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.600
For example, if you were compressing a 4k
image, you could substantially reduce the

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.240
size of the file by halving its resolution,
but this is obviously not the best solution.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.110
Eugene’s goal was to create a lossless data
compression algorithm and this led him to

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.720
creating the RAR file format.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.390
But, wait a minute, how does data compression
work.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.460
How can you store the same data within a smaller
space without losing any data?

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.820
Well, the key is to leverage natural redundancies
within the data itself.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.840
For example, let’s take a piece of text.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.950
If you wanted to save this quote onto your
computer, the most rudimentary method would

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.590
be to go letter by letter.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.130
You could tell the computer that the first
characer is I and the second character is

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.620
t and the third character is a space and so
on and so forth.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.340
But, a far more efficient method would be
tell the computer that character in the 1,17,

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.180
23, 31, and 34 positions are all the letter
i.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.380
Now of course, that’s an extremely basic
form of data compression, but that’s the

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.690
overall premise.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:03.340
The idea is to eliminate redundant data storage
as much as possible by using algorithms, machine

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.590
learning, partial matching etc.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.980
Anyway, after grinding for quite some time,
Eugene came up with a new file format called

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.600
RAR in 1993.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.180
And, two years later, he would release a piece
of software that could compress data into

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.920
and out of the RAR file format, and this marked
the birth of WinRAR.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.530
Given that the software was free to try, it
didn’t take long for WinRAR to gain popularity

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.349
within the computer market.

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:28.319
After all, most users were desperate for storage.

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:32.340
This isn’t nearly as relatable nowadays
given that we have loads of storage.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.340
Not to mention, external storage options as
well as cloud storage options are both cheaper

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.770
than ever.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.230
And this is one of the main reasons that the
popularity of WinRAR has been on a massive

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.040
decline over the past decade.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.709
But, back in the day, computers had extremely
limited storage, so you had to be super careful

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:49.099
with what you stored.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:53.160
Anyway, Eugene continuously launched newer
versions of WinRAR that implemented the latest

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:57.739
data compression technology, and overtime
RAR along with ZIP became the standard for

00:03:57.739 --> 00:03:59.830
file compression.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:05.000
FREE:
Alright, so now that we discussed the history

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.989
of WinRAR, let’s take a look the paid version
of WinRAR.

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:12.950
More specifically, let’s look at what exactly
the paid version offers over the free version.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.931
By far the biggest benefit that you get from
paying for WinRAR is that you don’t have

00:04:16.931 --> 00:04:21.810
to close the buy page window everytime you
open WinRAR, but that’s pretty much it.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.909
You’re basically paying $29 to remove this
pesky window and of course be on the right

00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:28.020
side of the moral compass.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.150
This may not sound like a big perk at first
glance, but if you use WinRAR on a regular

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:35.620
basis, you’ll spend hours closing these
pesky windows throughout your lifetime.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.169
A fellow YouTuber named RMD tech actually
has a full break down of how much time you

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:44.259
can save by purchasing the license and why
it’s well worth the investment.

00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:48.499
Jokes aside though, no one gives a crap about
having to close this window.

00:04:48.499 --> 00:04:51.879
Not to mention there are plenty of easy ways
to get rid of this window without paying not

00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:54.539
that I recommend any of them of course.

00:04:54.539 --> 00:04:59.069
But realistically, the entire program is basically
free for everyday people.

00:04:59.069 --> 00:05:02.930
This might sound like a stupid business decision,
but there’s actually multiple legitimate

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.919
reasons behind this starting with popularity.

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:09.900
It is possible to make software hard enough
to deter the vast majority of pirates.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.090
I mean just take a look at Mac OS.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.189
Yes, it is possible to install Mac OS on a
windows machine, but actually getting through

00:05:17.189 --> 00:05:21.250
all of the kernel errors and driver issues
takes well over 10 hours, and most people

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.580
just don’t care enough to go through that.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.699
The thing to note, however, is that this does
not mean that this user is just not gonna

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:29.389
use a computer at all.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:33.639
Chances are, they’re just gonna pirate a
piece of software that’s easier to pirate.

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:37.889
Within the OS space, this was Windows, and
this is one of the main reasons that Windows

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:39.290
is so popular.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:42.919
If Windows wasn’t easy to pirate, it’s
very possible that the dominant OS would’ve

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:47.339
been Linux, but since Windows was easy, most
people just stopped right there.

00:05:47.339 --> 00:05:50.210
This same rule applies to WinRAR as well.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.779
If it was hard to pirate WinRAR, people would’ve
likely just used Zip instead, or maybe some

00:05:54.779 --> 00:05:56.949
other file format would’ve risen to the
top.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:00.740
So, offering WinRAR for free to the public
was basically an investment for the company

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.779
to get the public on their side.

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:06.779
Once they have the public on their side, they’ve
basically already won because companies will

00:06:06.779 --> 00:06:07.800
have to follow suit.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.860
Now of course, there’s gonna be some oddball
companies out their that force customers to

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:13.750
use some obscure file format.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:17.539
But the vast majority will just adopt what
the public is already used to.

00:06:17.539 --> 00:06:19.819
This doesn’t just apply to customers either.

00:06:19.819 --> 00:06:24.060
Employees are also part of the public, and
if WinRAR is what employees are most familiar

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.770
with, that’s most likely what they’re
gonna use.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.520
This is the key reason that software like
Microsoft Office and Adobe’s Creative Suite

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.380
are so common amongst companies and schools.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:38.669
If you get the public on your side, institutions
will follow, and unlike the public, the instutitons

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:40.449
will actually pay.

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:46.089
WHY EVEN PAY:
As an everyday person, there’s really no

00:06:46.089 --> 00:06:50.029
reason to pay for WinRAR unless you simply
want to support the developers.

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:51.029
And if that’s you, that’s great.

00:06:51.029 --> 00:06:56.389
But, at the same time, you don’t really
risk anything by not paying.

00:06:56.389 --> 00:06:58.680
This is not the same case with companies though.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.349
Sure, it wouldn’t be that hard for companies
to just pirate like everyone else, but this

00:07:03.349 --> 00:07:04.869
is simply not worth the consequences.

00:07:04.869 --> 00:07:08.120
And I’m not talking about legal consequences.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.180
Sure, WinRAR could sue you and you’ll have
go through the hassle of a lawsuit, but that’s

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.900
not the biggest concern.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.259
The biggest concern is the reputational risk
your taking by pirating software, and that’s

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:22.620
simply not worth it especially given how cheap
WinRAR is.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:27.379
If you’re buying licenses for over a thousand
employees, you only have to pay a measly $6

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:28.560
per license.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.779
If it get’s leaked, however, that your company
has been using pirated software, you’ll

00:07:31.779 --> 00:07:33.960
be paying a far greater price.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.750
For example, let’s say Apple needs to use
WinRAR for whatever reason.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:42.210
Apple currently has about 150,000 employees,
and let’s say 10,000 of these employees

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.479
need access to WinRAR.

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:47.529
At $6 per license, that’s a lifetime cost
of $60,000.

00:07:47.529 --> 00:07:52.259
Now, let’s say Apple tried to avoid this
cost and pirated WinRAR and that this eventually

00:07:52.259 --> 00:07:53.259
got leaked.

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:57.309
Surely, there will be people who will no longer
be willing to shop at Apple because they don’t

00:07:57.309 --> 00:08:00.779
want to support a mega cap that’s actively
screwing over a small business.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:05.409
Out of the hundred million phones Apple sells
every year, even if only 100 customers make

00:08:05.409 --> 00:08:10.419
this choice, Apple would lose out on $80,000
worth of revenue at $800 per phone.

00:08:10.419 --> 00:08:13.840
Now, what if a 1000 customers decided to stop
shopping at Apple.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.339
Then we’re looking at an $800,000 revenue
hit.

00:08:16.339 --> 00:08:20.279
At 10,000 customers, we’re looking at an
$8 million revenue hit.

00:08:20.279 --> 00:08:25.430
Keep in mind that 10,000 phones is just 0.01%
of Apple’s annual volume.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:31.669
So, even if pirating WinRAR only pissed off
0.01% of just their iPhone customer base,

00:08:31.669 --> 00:08:34.300
Apple would be missing out on millions of
dollars worth of revenue.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:38.070
And we’re not even considering recurring
revenue from these customers.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.690
This issue gets exponentially worse if we’re
talking about a software company.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:45.540
For example, what if it got leaked that Adobe
or Microsoft weren’t paying for WinRAR?

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:49.779
In this scenario, the company that’s trying
to sell you software isn’t paying for software

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:51.100
themselves.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:54.200
What better pass do customers need to just
pirate the company’s software.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:58.180
At this point, even customers who would generally
pay for the software would consider pirating

00:08:58.180 --> 00:08:59.180
it.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.360
Clearly, this would not be a very favorable
outcome, and it’s more than enough motivation

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.519
for companies to simply pay for all their
software.

00:09:07.519 --> 00:09:12.779
THE FREE GODFATHER:
In the end, WinRAR was basically the godfather

00:09:12.779 --> 00:09:14.820
of the freemium business model.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:18.380
Just get users on your side and then figure
out monetization later.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:22.470
When they first leveraged this model in the
1990s, it was quite a bold move.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:26.870
But, today, many of the businesses that we
come across on a daily basis use this same

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:27.870
model.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:32.750
I mean, when was the last time you paid Instagram
or Facebook or Google or Snapchat.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.730
None of these companies have a pop up window
that tells you that the service is not free

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:38.320
and that you have to pay for it.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.820
But at the core, the all make money in similar
manners.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.880
Once they have you on their side, they offer
some sort of service to companies, and it’s

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:47.589
the companies that pay for you.

00:09:47.589 --> 00:09:52.089
Nowadays, this model has actually become so
popular that it’s unique if you don’t

00:09:52.089 --> 00:09:53.089
leverage it.

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:57.050
For example, the selling point of newer search
engines and social media platforms is that

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.710
they don’t collect your data.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:03.089
They’re either decentralized non-profit
organizations or they directly charge customers

00:10:03.089 --> 00:10:04.519
for their use.

00:10:04.519 --> 00:10:06.410
YouTube premium is a perfect example of this.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:09.670
It doesn’t look like WinRAR is trying to
hop onto this new trend though.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:14.610
They’ve become iconic for their 40 day trial
period that’s actually infinite, and I don’t

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.149
think they’re looking to change that.

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:20.940
Also, just based on Google Trends, it looks
like WinRAR is on its way out as the demand

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.009
for file compression amongst everyday people
is slowly dying off.

00:10:24.009 --> 00:10:25.450
This isn’t to say that WinRAR is gonna shutdown
per se.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.811
According to this website, they still pull
in $21 million worth of revenue, and given

00:10:28.811 --> 00:10:32.000
that they run an extremely lean operation,
they’re likely still profiting millions

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:33.000
per year.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.390
So, while they probably won't become the next
Adobe or anything, it looks like they’ve

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:40.740
got a solid business that works for them and
that wont be changing anytime soon.

